## What I did

Worked the top-ranked defect, **D-FOREIGN-REPLAY-UNGATED-IMAGES**, step 5.3 — item (2) of the sess98 list: the **acquire half** of the sess96 RULE-5 ruling (gen snapshot + install from the granting CAS's own result).

**Landed — 0.11.432, `srcversion 619FFAF4EB078599367EFA5`, builds clean, not deployed, not rig-verified.**

The structural finding that made this tractable: `mxfs_dlm_inode_lock_routed()` is a real chokepoint — both the iclus-routed and per-inode arms funnel through it, so one edit covers every acquire that can establish authority. It now takes the gen snapshot as a parameter and does routing-revoke → install under `i_dlm_lock`. `mxfs_dlm_ilock_begin` samples the gen at the `acq_inflight++`/ACQUIRING transition, which is genuinely *before* descending into the DLM — that ordering is what makes the re-check a stale-completion guard rather than decoration.

Two decisions worth flagging:

- **The ICLUSTER fast path runs no CAS at all**, so most cluster-routed acquires had no result to install. I added `ic->auth_epoch`, set by the granting CAS in the same `ic->lock` section that raises `disk_mode` and zeroed in that same section at all three release sites. That is a *continuity* argument, not the resource-keyed cache the ruling rejected: `disk_mode > NL` holds for exactly the interval between the two, both serialised by `ic->lock`, so one locked read is first-hand evidence of a continuously-held tenure.
- **The routing-flip revoke absorbs only its own gen bump** (`mine = (gen == gen_snap)` before revoking). Unconditional re-snapshotting would erase a *third party's* relinquishment and let a stale completion install.

**Gap I found and deliberately did not paper over:** in the routed arm the unpublished de-list happens after the lock section, so install refuses the very unpublished→durable promotion it was meant to enable. Moving the de-list earlier flips the `conv_pi` predicate and costs a wasted device CAS per create — the wrong fix. The right one is nesting `i_dlm_lock` → `m_mxfs_unpub_lock`, which I verified by grep is an unused ordering today (all 14 `m_mxfs_unpub_lock` sites drop `i_dlm_lock` first).

Also recorded: the re-affirm path must land **before** wiring `begin_release`, or sticky RELEASING creates a large permanently-non-proving population; and sessions 95–98 have all shipped build-clean-but-unmeasured code, so the counter debugfs + a deploy is now the highest-value next move.

